{"id": "79862", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=79862", "title": "Batavia (New York)", "text": "Batavia is a city and town in Genesee County, New York.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nBatavia is almost impossible to miss if you're driving anywhere near Genesee County. As the oldest population center in this part of the state, it was only natural for major roadways to be built radiating outward from here. Sure enough, four different state routes all pass through the city, all meeting at the intersection of Main and Oak (and three of them concurrently occupy a small stretch of West Main). New York State Route 5 is a major east-west route through the state, from the Pennsylvania border all the way to Albany. Route 33 runs between Buffalo and Rochester. Route 63 starts up in Lyndonville, and runs southeast through Batavia (skirting Tonawanda Creek) to Geneseo and past Dansville. Finally, Route 98 runs from Lake Ontario's Point Breeze in the north all the way down to the Southern Tier just outside Salamanca.\nOh, and the New York State Thruway (I-90) also passes just north of the city. You know, the major cross-state expressway. It's a toll road, but it's unquestionably the fastest way to get to Batavia; take exit 48 (for Route 98).\nBy bus.\nBatavia is served by Greyhound, Flixbus, and Trailways buses.\nBy plane.\nIf you'd prefer to fly, Genesee County Airport (FAA LID: GVQ) sits just north of the Thruway off of Route 98. There are no scheduled commercial flights, but it's got a mile-long runway and about a hundred private planes come and go each day\nThe nearest commercial airports are in Buffalo and Rochester, both 27 miles away.\nGet around.\nRTS Genesee (formerly Batavia Bus Service, or the B line) takes people around the region. It goes all over the place, and even takes people to the community college."}
{"id": "79863", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=79863", "title": "Le Roy (New York)", "text": "Le Roy is a town in Genesee County, New York. It contains the village of Le Roy and the hamlets of Fort Hill and Lime Rock.\nUnderstand.\nLe Roy is located along Interstate 90 (the New York State Thruway), near the western terminus of Interstate 490 (an auxiliary freeway that heads into the city of Rochester). From I-90, exit on to I-490 (exit #47) and then take the first exit (NY Route 19) to reach Le Roy."}
{"id": "79864", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=79864", "title": "Victor (New York)", "text": "Victor is a town in Ontario County, New York. It includes the village of Victor and the hamlets of Boughton Hill, East Victor, Fishers, Motts Corners, and Railroad Mills.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nVictor is on Interstate 90 (the New York State Thruway), near the eastern terminus of Interstate 490 (an auxiliary freeway that heads into the city of Rochester).\nBuy.\nShopping in Victor is dominated by the enormous shopping mall in the northeast corner of the town. There are a number of large strip malls surrounding it, too. But head into the village and you'll find an array of small-town independent shops as well."}
{"id": "79865", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=79865", "title": "Henrietta", "text": "Henrietta is a town in Monroe County, New York. It contains the hamlets of East Henrietta and West Henrietta.\nUnderstand.\nHenrietta is home to the Rochester Institute of Technology (RIT).\nGet in.\nBy car.\nHenrietta is at the junction of Interstate 90 (the New York State Thruway) and Interstate 390.\nGet around.\nOn foot.\nLike many American suburbs, Henrietta is very car-centric. Pedestrian travel isn't generally advisable due to the long distances involved.\nBy bike.\nSurprisingly possible depending upon where you are going. There is a network of trails that keeps you away from the main roads.\nBy car.\nA car is easily the best way to get around Henrietta. Although traffic is heavy, the roads are fairly wide so you won't sit in traffic much."}
{"id": "79881", "revid": "2387180", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=79881", "title": "Brunswick Land", "text": "The Brunswick Land \"(Braunschweiger Land)\" is in Lower Saxony \"(Niedersachsen)\", Germany between L\u00fcneburg Heath and Harz.\nUnderstand.\nThe region is a fascinating mix of modern high-tech industry and deep medieval roots. As the former heart of the Guelph (Welfen) dynasty, it boasts impressive half-timbered towns, while also being home to the global headquarters of Volkswagen.\nGet around.\nThe region is well-connected by regional trains (RB/RE). While the Niedersachsen-Ticket is a cost-effective choice for small groups or single-day trips, travelers staying longer or traveling solo across Germany should consider the Deutschlandticket. It offers unlimited regional travel for a flat monthly fee, making it the better value for extensive itineraries."}
{"id": "79882", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=79882", "title": "Hanover Region", "text": "Hanover Region (German: \"Region Hannover\") is in the state of Lower Saxony in Germany.\nGet in.\nSection \"Get in\" for Hanover provides useful information to start with.\nGet around.\nBy public transportation.\nMost of the Hanover Region belongs to the Gro\u00dfraum-Verkehr Hannover (GVH) transport system that includes local trains, bus, tram and (underground) light rail. The Verden district, however, is part of the Bremen-centred Verkehrsverbund Bremen/Niedersachsen (VBN) network, while Nienburg and Hildesheim districts each organise their own local transport, not participating in either regional association.\nIf you plan to make more than two trips by said means of transport within the GVH area, a day ticket is your best bet, buying you unlimited travel on the public transport system. Day tickets are valid until the last connection of the day (which normally ends sometime after midnight). The public transportation adopts an honor system for tickets, but they do have ticket inspections once in a while (also late at nights and early mornings). The fine is quite high, so make sure you have a ticket all the time. Be aware that some tickets will need to be validated (stamped on the 'blue box'), and some not, depending on the machine.\n* Day ticket: (1 zone), (2 zones), (3 zones)\n* Group day ticket (up to 5 persons): (1 zone), (2 zones), (3 zones)\n* Single-trip ticket: (1 zone), (2 zones), (3 zones)\n* Short-trip ticket (up to 3 stops for tram and 5 stops for bus): \n* Reduced ticket (children 6-14y, big dogs): (all zone)\n* Special price for a carnet of 6 single-trip tickets (\"SammelTicket\"): \u20ac14.00 (1 zone) and \u20ac17.50 (2 zones) resp. 4 single-trip tickets \u20ac14.90 (3 zones)\nNote: If you are a returning visitor and have any carnets or day tickets left: Bring them along ! All tickets issued after 01-JAN-2002 (= those with \u20ac as currency) have unlimited validity. "}
{"id": "79911", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=79911", "title": "Taunus", "text": "The Taunus is a low mountain range in South Hesse, Middle Hesse and the Rhineland-Palatinate in southern Germany.\nUnderstand.\nIt is a relatively low range, with smooth, rounded mountains that are covered with forest. The Taunus is bounded by the river valleys of the Rhine, Main and Lahn rivers and it is part of the Rhenish Slate Mountains. On the opposite side of the Middle Rhine Valley, The Taunus range is continued by the Hunsr\u00fcck.\nGet around.\nTravelling by bus and rail on RMV services[dead link]"}
