{"id": "110960", "revid": "2432955", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=110960", "title": "Westport (Ontario)", "text": "Westport is a tranquil village of 700 people in the Rideau Lakes at the west end of Upper Rideau Lake, the head of navigation for the Rideau Canal system.\nUnderstand.\nInitial settlement began between 1810 and 1820, with Westport incorporated as an independent municipality in 1904. By 1829, Westport was the site of a saw mill and grist mill; the construction of the Rideau Canal in the mid-1800s contributed to the growth of the village as a local commercial centre. Westport received many Irish immigrants following the Great Famine (1840s through 1860s); St. Edward's Catholic Church, built in 1859 at Concession and Bedford, served as a community centre. A railway ran from Brockville (and the St. Lawrence River) to Westport from 1888 to 1952. A provincial fish sanctuary was established at the Westport Pond in 1957.\nGet in.\nBy motorcar:\nUpper Rideau Lake is north of Newboro lock station on the Rideau Canal; Westport is at the northwest corner of the lake. Westport Harbour's has docks for thirty boats, a visitor's centre and a picnic area.\nWestport and the Foley Mountain Conservation Area are part of the Rideau Trail, a hiking trail from Kingston to Ottawa.\nThere is no scheduled bus or rail service in Westport village. The last train ran from Brockville to Westport in 1952. There is a tiny airstrip for private planes, but the closest (limited) passenger services are in Kingston.\nGet around.\nIt is possible to dock a small boat / kayak at Westport Harbour's marina and then walk to destinations within the village.\nConversely, one can drive to Westport, park in the village and rent a boat to go onto the lakes:\nSee.\nhttps://www.facebook.com/norriss.livebait/\nNearby.\nNewboro.\nTiny village 10km (6mi) east of Westport, at the highest point on the Rideau Canal (lock #36 is directly south of the village)."}
{"id": "110973", "revid": "2196717", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=110973", "title": "Columbia Falls", "text": "Columbia Falls is a town in northwest Montana. About 5,300 people lived there in 2020. The town is only 10 miles from Kalispell and 17 miles from Glacier National Park making it incredibly popular for tourism. This article also includes the nearby community of Hungry Horse.\nGet in.\nU.S. Route 2 (US-2) runs through the center of Columbia Falls. It is about 10 miles east of US-93.\nThe nearest airport is Glacier Park International Airport (), 8 miles to the south.\nBuy.\nSummer brings the most people so some businesses will be closed, or have reduced hours, during winter months."}
{"id": "110984", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=110984", "title": "Levallois-Perret", "text": "Levallois-Perret is a wealthy suburb northwest of Paris.\nGet in.\nBy M\u00e9tro.\nLine 3 of the Paris M\u00e9tro ends in Levallois-Perret.\nSee.\nThe town includes many monuments listed in the general inventory of the cultural heritage of France. The historical monuments of the town are:\nEat.\nLe bistrot de Lola, the oldest bistro in Levallois-Perret"}
{"id": "110990", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=110990", "title": "Polson", "text": "Polson is a town of 5,100 people (2020) in Montana, on the Flathead Indian Reservation. It is at the south end of Flathead Lake, the largest natural freshwater lake west of the Mississippi.\nGet in.\nPolson is on U.S. Highway 93 (US-93), an hour north of Missoula and an hour south of Kalispell.\nFrom Interstate 90, take exit 96.\nGet around.\nWhile Polson is a smaller town, most of the businesses are along the highway. There isn't a centralized \"downtown\" shop area so using a car or bicycle is best."}
